                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                        "entry_id": "a8456f9a-6941-3990-abee-c007ac140b2e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "lijkend op klei, vochtig, glad, koud"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5934de6c-9a48-4138-b6d1-cf39230a45ce",
                                "sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                                "text": "'Lekker veel tijd voor jezelf' is egoïsme verpakt in Happinezz-gelul. Teamsporten vormen niet alleen maatschappelijke lijm, maar kweken ook karakters. Wie ooit op zaterdagochtend met zeurende koppijn op een kleiig veld de huid is volgescholden na een slechte pass vraagt zich af: wat doe ik hier? Twee uur later met een biertje in de hand weet je dat er niets mooiers is. HP de Tijd 29/07"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2639045f-041f-417b-b847-946bc5a70b99",
                                "sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                                "text": "Hoe smaakt het water in de grachten, sloten en vaarten van Amsterdam? Nou, naar ijsthee, naar niks, zoutig of 'kleiïg' [sic!]- het is maar net waar je een slok neemt. In de Gaasperplas is het water het schoonst."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ad3354d-ba2f-4810-af7e-239739ba8c9d",
                                "sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                                "text": "„Pas op, het is hier dieper dan je denkt.” Ecoloog Johan Krol steekt een kleiige kreek over, het water staat bijna tot aan de laarsrand."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "931ae0ac-15cd-4f66-a873-88f80b91894e",
                        "source_entry_id": "a8456f9a-6941-3990-abee-c007ac140b2e",
                        "source_sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                        "source_sense_description": "kleiig",
                        "target_lang_word": "argillaceous",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "argillaceous",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b899c272-9a04-382d-88be-18f5bb71dae7",
                                        "entry_id": "7b62cde7-87b6-35eb-afb8-1f0136733b4c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Pertaining to clay; made of, containing, or resembling clay; clayey."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8fcea7f9-358b-4365-ba63-19241f813591",
                                                "sense_id": "b899c272-9a04-382d-88be-18f5bb71dae7",
                                                "text": "[…] natural colossi from two to five hundred feet high, done in argillaceous sandstone or a singular species of conglomerate, all of which owe their existence almost entirely to the agency of wind."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0703ddd1-2feb-4d92-85ad-b140669f1b6b",
                                                "sense_id": "b899c272-9a04-382d-88be-18f5bb71dae7",
                                                "text": "The gleam of the land is in its rocks, the fine-grained argillaceous rocks, here, not purple or grey, but green of living stone."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8d891c7f-0feb-4f70-87c7-22a57cd1bd2d",
                        "source_entry_id": "a8456f9a-6941-3990-abee-c007ac140b2e",
                        "source_sense_id": "5109971b-cadb-3c87-95b5-57269a1f7bb6",
                        "source_sense_description": "kleiig",
                        "target_lang_word": "clayey",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "clayey",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2e1d0f94-77bf-3889-a303-8a4c475a3c51",
                                        "entry_id": "70d39c8a-6ef1-37f6-ac15-cf814087449b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Composed of clay or containing (much) clay; clayish."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0ff9b3ab-1aa0-4c38-9389-8a03b8b8aebb",
                                                "sense_id": "2e1d0f94-77bf-3889-a303-8a4c475a3c51",
                                                "text": "The shores of the rivers and creeks are chiefly planted with coffee, to the distance of about 30 miles from the sea; thence 30 miles farther up, the soil becomes clayey and more fit for sugar[-]canes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "30e56b99-f34c-4904-90f2-040d7b3c6654",
                                                "sense_id": "2e1d0f94-77bf-3889-a303-8a4c475a3c51",
                                                "text": "She had walked over rotted, decaying, splintered planks covered with clayey soil: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "917eb1b8-19e2-4c5a-9be7-8a23126e0872",
                                                "sense_id": "2e1d0f94-77bf-3889-a303-8a4c475a3c51",
                                                "text": "Limestone, of course, is calcium carbonate, and thus chemically utterly different in composition from the clayey rocks below and the hard, pebbly ones above."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0a8cb9be-f1bc-3324-87d4-249ad88329fd",
                                        "entry_id": "70d39c8a-6ef1-37f6-ac15-cf814087449b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Covered or dirtied with clay."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "abf15060-5b88-4cba-a5a1-b389bb596fba",
                                                "sense_id": "0a8cb9be-f1bc-3324-87d4-249ad88329fd",
                                                "text": "Wheat-fields, one would think, cannot come to grow untilled; no man made clayey, or made weary thereby;—unless machinery will do it?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "022e6799-f67e-3458-867e-b5fb27ff9079",
                                        "entry_id": "70d39c8a-6ef1-37f6-ac15-cf814087449b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Resembling clay; claylike, clayish."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "82de5875-2569-4caa-9fef-23e22e3a84d2",
                                                "sense_id": "022e6799-f67e-3458-867e-b5fb27ff9079",
                                                "text": "Death, grim Death, will fold / Me, in his leaden Arms, and preſs me cloſe / To his cold clayie Breaſt: […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1af28081-16b1-3902-9623-9c4a9cb28e7a",
                                        "entry_id": "70d39c8a-6ef1-37f6-ac15-cf814087449b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of the human body, as contrasted with the soul; bodily, human, mortal."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "069832ed-3f22-477b-84da-b601044f9d76",
                                                "sense_id": "1af28081-16b1-3902-9623-9c4a9cb28e7a",
                                                "text": "This purifing of wit, this enritching of memory, enabling of iudgment, and enlarging of conceyt, which commonly we call learning, […] the final end is, to lead and draw vs to as high a perfection, as our degenerate ſoules made worſe by their clayey lodgings, can be capable of."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bd9eaa8b-b139-4257-86e3-8f9c06d4215f",
                                                "sense_id": "1af28081-16b1-3902-9623-9c4a9cb28e7a",
                                                "text": "[A]mid these tombs, / Cold as their clayey tenants, know, my heart / Must never grow to stone!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2159ecb7-76f1-448a-946a-12493aa2f7f7",
                                                "sense_id": "1af28081-16b1-3902-9623-9c4a9cb28e7a",
                                                "text": "To low estate of clayey creature, / See, I bring the beggar's meed, / Nutriment beyond the need!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "78f08303-028d-436d-8108-362e4c752c77",
                                                "sense_id": "1af28081-16b1-3902-9623-9c4a9cb28e7a",
                                                "text": "[W]hen between sheets, whether by day or by night, and whether asleep or awake, I have a way of always keeping my eyes shut, in order the more to concentrate the snugness of being in bed. Because no man can ever feel his own identity aright except his eyes be closed; as if, darkness were indeed the proper element of our essences, though light be more congenial to our clayey part."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
